course: dictionary
lesson: 4062
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word gent is one of them. Context, meaning the words around it, tells you which meaning the writer wants.
explain: «He held the door open, tipped his hat, and said, "After you." What a real gent!» Here a gent is a polite, well-dressed man. The politeness in the sentence is the clue.
explain: «Ladies and gents, please take your seats.» This time gents means men, because it sits next to ladies. Gents is an informal word, so people use it in friendly talk, not in formal writing.
fact: A gent is an informal, old-fashioned word for a gentleman.
fact: «A real gent» means a polite, well-dressed man.
fact: «The Gents» is an informal British word for the men's public toilet.
fact: «Ladies and gents» means women and men, and it is said to a group.
fact: On signs, «gents'» means for men, as in «gents' hairdresser».
exercise: «He held the door for her and tipped his hat, a real gent.» What does gent mean here? | A large animal | *A polite, well-dressed man | A school bell | A kind of cake
exercise: On a sign in a British station you read «Gents» next to «Ladies». What does it mean? | The café | The train platform | The ticket office | *The men's toilet
exercise: «Ladies and gents, please take your seats.» Who is being spoken to? | Only old people | *Women and men | Only children | Only dogs
test: «Gents' hairdresser» means what? | *A hairdresser for men | A hairdresser for children | A hairdresser for pets | A shop that sells hats
test: Why might someone call a friend «a proper gent»? | Because he is famous on television | Because he is very loud | Because he is ten years old | *Because he is kind and polite
exam: When you meet gent and don't know which meaning is right, what should you do first? | Skip the whole text and never come back | Look only at the first letter | Guess it is a type of food | *Read the words around it and see which meaning fits
